What “fat loss stalls” actually means

A stall is not:

One week with no change

Daily scale fluctuations

Temporary water retention

A hard training week or higher sodium intake

A stall is:

No downward trend in body weight or measurements

For 2–3 consecutive weeks

While calories, training, and adherence are consistent

Why 2–3 weeks (the physiology)

Body weight is influenced by:

Glycogen storage

Water retention

Digestion content

Hormonal fluctuations

These can mask fat loss for 7–14 days, even when fat is still being lost.

Waiting 2–3 weeks ensures:

Water noise has settled

True energy balance is revealed

You avoid unnecessary calorie cuts

How to assess a stall correctly (practical method)
Step 1: Weigh-in protocol

Weigh yourself daily

Same time each morning

Same conditions (after bathroom, before food)

Step 2: Use averages

Calculate a 7-day rolling average

Compare week-to-week averages, not single days

Step 3: Decision rule

If:

Week 1 average = 80.0 kg

Week 2 average = 79.9 kg

Week 3 average = 80.0 kg

→ That is a stall

If:

Week 1 = 80.0

Week 2 = 79.6

Week 3 = 79.4

→ Fat loss is still occurring

When you actually adjust calories

Only adjust after:

≥ 14–21 days

Verified adherence (no hidden calories)

Stable training volume

How to adjust (conservative)

Reduce 100–150 kcal/day, not more

Or add 1–2k steps/day

Never slash protein or fiber

This prevents:

Muscle loss

Hormonal disruption

Rebound hunger

